  "query_preview": "..\n\nTonight through Thursday: Broad low pressure centered off the \nOregon coast tracks inland between this evening and Thursday \nnight. Moisture overrunning a deformation axis has been allowing\nthe next swath of middle to high clouds to increase over the \nregion. That feature will continue to edge north, lowering \nclouds and increasing precipitation across some of the region. \nThe best chances start near the Cascades through southeast WA \nand expand north and east between late evening into the \novernight. Then the precipitation chances start to decrease from\nthe west Thursday afternoon. \n\nThe easterly flow will provide some upslope into the western \nbasin and the Cascades. Temperatures there have been holding in \nthe mid to upper 30s in that area; so a rain-snow mix is \nforecast in the valleys, with all snow above about 2500-3500 \nfeet. Some heavy snow is possible in some of the mountain areas,\nlike Blewett Pass. A winter weather advisory is in effect from \nthis afternoon through late Thursday morning that includes \nBlewett Pass and Mission Ridge, with accumulations of 4-8 inches\nexpected, but local accumulations of 10 inches or more will be \npossible. We will have to keep an eye on the nearby valleys and \nwestern Waterville Plateau for some light accumulations as well \nand if it starts to accumulate in a more significant way, we \nwill consider other highlights. Snow is also forecast in the \nother mountains, while rain or a rain/snow mix is possible \nelsewhere. Lows tonight are forecast to be in the upper 20s to \nlower 30s. Highs will be in the upper 30s and 40s Thursday.\n\nFriday to Saturday: the area briefly moves into a quieter \npattern, with weak high pressure building in. A westerly flow \nwill keep some limited mountain shower chances alive, but \notherwise it will be dry. We will have to watch for some \nmorning/late night fog. Expect highs in the upper 30s to lower \n40s, with low in the mid to upper 20s to low 30s.\n\nSaturday night through Wednesday: Another low approaches \nSaturday, taking a similar track to the one coming this evening,\ncoming to the Oregon coast and moving inland through the week. \nIt seems slow to move in. Cooler temperatures are expected and \nany precipitation is expected to fall as snow for much of the \nlowlands. The uncertainty is how much snow: a dusting or \nsomething more measurable. So impacts are possible in the \nlowlands. A better risk for snow accumulations comes to the \nmountains. We are keeping an eye Wednesday for possible moderate\nsnow amounts over the Cascade passes, but right now models show\nabout a 30 to 60 percent chance of 1 inch of snow (highest near\nStevens Pass) and 20 percent chance of 3 inches of snow over \nBlewett, Stevens and Washington Pass by then. Lows are forecast \nto be in the mid-20s to lower 30s through Tuesday, moderating \ntoward the upper 20s to mid-30s by Wednesday. Highs are forecast\nto be in the mid to upper 30s Sunday, moderating toward the \nupper 30s to mid-40s Tuesday and Wednesday. /Solveig",
